      "question": "A6. Let $k \\geqslant 2$ be an integer. Determine all sequences of positive integers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots$ for which there exists a monic polynomial $P$ of degree $k$ with non-negative integer coefficients such that\n\n$$\nP\\left(a_{n}\\right)=a_{n+1} a_{n+2} \\cdots a_{n+k}\n$$\n\nfor every integer $n \\geqslant 1$.",
      "steps": [
        "Step 0: Let the monic polynomial be $P(x) = x^k + c_{k-1}x^{k-1} + \\dots + c_1x + c_0$, where $c_i$ are non-negative integers. The given relation is $P(a_n) = a_{n+1}a_{n+2}\\cdots a_{n+k}$ for all $n \\ge 1$. Since $a_n$ are positive integers, $a_n \\ge 1$. As all $c_i \\ge 0$, the polynomial $P(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x > 0$.\n\nFirst, we show that the sequence $(a_n)_{n \\ge 1}$ must be eventually non-decreasing.\nSuppose for some $n \\ge 1$, we have $a_{n+1} < a_n$. Since $P(x)$ is strictly increasing for positive values, $P(a_{n+1}) < P(a_n)$.\nThe given relation for $n$ and $n+1$ is:\n$P(a_n) = a_{n+1} a_{n+2} \\cdots a_{n+k}$\n$P(a_{n+1}) = a_{n+2} a_{n+3} \\cdots a_{n+k+1}$\nDividing these two equations, we get\n$$ \\frac{P(a_{n+1})}{P(a_n)} = \\frac{a_{n+k+1}}{a_{n+1}} $$\nSince $a_{n+1} < a_n$ implies $P(a_{n+1}) < P(a_n)$, we have $\\frac{P(a_{n+1})}{P(a_n)} < 1$.\nTherefore, $\\frac{a_{n+k+1}}{a_{n+1}} < 1$, which means $a_{n+k+1} < a_{n+1}$.\nThis shows that if $a_{n+1} < a_n$ for some $n$, then $a_{n+k+1} < a_{n+1}$. This generates a strictly decreasing subsequence $a_{n+1}, a_{n+k+1}, a_{n+2k+1}, \\ldots$. Since the terms of this subsequence are positive integers, this is impossible. Thus, our assumption must be false. There can only be a finite number of indices $n$ for which $a_{n+1} < a_n$.\nThis means there exists an integer $N$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, $a_{n+1} \\ge a_n$. The sequence is eventually non-decreasing.",
        "Step 1: We now analyze two cases based on the behavior of the sequence for $n \\ge N$.\n\nCase 1: The sequence is eventually constant.\nLet $a_n = a$ for all $n \\ge N$, where $a$ is a positive integer.\nFor any $n \\ge N$, the equation $P(a_n) = a_{n+1} \\cdots a_{n+k}$ becomes $P(a) = a^k$.\nSubstituting the definition of $P(x)$:\n$a^k + c_{k-1}a^{k-1} + \\dots + c_1a + c_0 = a^k$.\nThis simplifies to $c_{k-1}a^{k-1} + \\dots + c_1a + c_0 = 0$.\nSince $a \\ge 1$ and $c_i \\ge 0$ are integers, every term on the left side is non-negative. For their sum to be zero, each term must be zero: $c_i a^i = 0$ for $i=0, 1, \\ldots, k-1$.\nAs $a \\ge 1$, this implies $c_i=0$ for all $i=0, 1, \\ldots, k-1$.\nSo, the polynomial must be $P(x) = x^k$.\n\nNow we must check if any sequence that is eventually constant is a solution with $P(x)=x^k$. The condition becomes $a_n^k = a_{n+1} \\cdots a_{n+k}$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\nLet's assume $a_n=a$ for $n \\ge N$.\nFor $n=N-1$, we have $a_{N-1}^k = a_N a_{N+1} \\cdots a_{N+k-1} = a \\cdot a \\cdots a = a^k$.\nSince $a_{N-1}$ is a positive integer, $a_{N-1} = a$.\nBy backward induction, $a_{n}=a$ for all $n \\le N$.\nThus, the sequence must be constant for all $n \\ge 1$, i.e., $a_n=a$ for some positive integer $a$.\nFor any such sequence, $a_n=a$, we have $P(a_n)=P(a)=a^k$ and $a_{n+1}\\cdots a_{n+k} = a^k$, so the condition is satisfied.\nSo, all constant sequences $a_n = a$ where $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$ are solutions.",
        "Step 2: Case 2: The sequence is not eventually constant.\nSince the sequence is eventually non-decreasing, it must be strictly increasing for $n \\ge N'$ for some $N' \\ge N$. For $n \\ge N'$, we have $a_{n+1} \\ge a_n+1$. This implies $a_n \\to \\infty$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\n\nFor $n \\ge N'$, since $a_{m+1} \\ge a_m$ for $m \\ge n$, we have $a_{n+1} \\le a_{n+2} \\le \\dots \\le a_{n+k}$.\nFrom $P(a_n) = a_{n+1} \\cdots a_{n+k}$, we get $a_n^k \\le P(a_n) \\le a_{n+k}^k$ and $a_n^k \\le P(a_n) \\ge a_{n+1}^k$.\nThis gives $a_{n+1} \\le P(a_n)^{1/k} = (a_n^k + c_{k-1}a_n^{k-1} + \\dots + c_0)^{1/k}$.\n$a_{n+1} \\le a_n \\left(1 + \\frac{c_{k-1}}{a_n} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{a_n^2}\\right)\\right)^{1/k} = a_n \\left(1 + \\frac{c_{k-1}}{k a_n} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{a_n^2}\\right)\\right) = a_n + \\frac{c_{k-1}}{k} + O\\left(\\frac{1}{a_n}\\right)$.\nFor $n$ large enough, $a_{n+1} \\le a_n + \\frac{c_{k-1}}{k} + \\frac{1}{2}$. As $a_{n+1}$ and $a_n$ are integers, this implies $a_{n+1} - a_n \\le \\lfloor \\frac{c_{k-1}}{k} + \\frac{1}{2} \\rfloor$.\nLet $d_n = a_{n+1}-a_n$. For $n \\ge N'$, $d_n \\ge 1$ and is bounded.\n\nLet $a_{n+j} = a_n + \\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} d_{n+i}$.\n$P(a_n) = \\prod_{j=1}^k a_{n+j} = \\prod_{j=1}^k \\left(a_n + \\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} d_{n+i}\\right)$.\nExpanding the product on the right side as a polynomial in $a_n$:\n$\\prod_{j=1}^k \\left(a_n + \\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} d_{n+i}\\right) = a_n^k + \\left(\\sum_{j=1}^k \\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} d_{n+i}\\right) a_n^{k-1} + O(a_n^{k-2})$.\nComparing the coefficient of $a_n^{k-1}$ with that of $P(a_n)=a_n^k+c_{k-1}a_n^{k-1}+\\dots$, we get:\n$c_{k-1} = \\sum_{j=1}^k \\sum_{i=0}^{j-1} d_{n+i} = k d_n + (k-1)d_{n+1} + \\dots + 1 \\cdot d_{n+k-1}$.\nThis relation must hold for all $n \\ge N'$. Thus, for $n \\ge N'$:\n$k d_n + (k-1)d_{n+1} + \\dots + d_{n+k-1} = k d_{n+1} + (k-1)d_{n+2} + \\dots + d_{n+k}$.\nThis gives a linear recurrence relation for the sequence $(d_n)$:\n$k d_n = d_{n+1} + d_{n+2} + \\dots + d_{n+k}$.\nThe characteristic equation is $r^k + r^{k-1} + \\dots + r - k = 0$.\nLet $f(r) = r^k + \\dots + r - k$. $f(1)=1+\\dots+1-k=0$, so $r=1$ is a root.\nFor $r>0$, $f'(r) = kr^{k-1} + (k-1)r^{k-2} + \\dots + 1 > 0$, so $f(r)$ is strictly increasing. Thus, $r=1$ is the unique positive real root.\nFor any complex root $r$ with $|r| > 1$, by the triangle inequality:\n$k = |r^k + \\dots + r| \\le |r|^k + \\dots + |r|$.\nLet $x=|r|>1$. Then $x^k+\\dots+x-k > 1^k+\\dots+1-k = 0$, so this is not a contradiction.\nHowever, we have $|\\sum_{j=1}^k r^j| \\le \\sum_{j=1}^k |r|^j$. Equality holds if $r, r^2, \\ldots, r^k$ are all positive real numbers, which implies $r$ is a positive real number. Since $r=1$ is the only positive real root, any root $r \\neq 1$ must satisfy $|\\sum_{j=1}^k r^j| < \\sum_{j=1}^k |r|^j$.\nIf $|r| \\ge 1$ and $r \\neq 1$, then $k=|\\sum_{j=1}^k r^j| < \\sum_{j=1}^k |r|^j$. Let $g(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^k x^j - k$ for $x \\ge 0$. $g(1)=0$ and $g'(x)>0$ for $x>0$. So for $|r|>1$, $\\sum_{j=1}^k |r|^j > k$. For $|r|=1, r\\neq 1$, we have strict inequality in triangle inequality, so $|\\sum_{j=1}^k r^j| < k$. Thus no roots with $|r|=1, r\\neq 1$.\nSo all roots other than $r=1$ must have absolute value strictly less than 1.\nThe general solution to the recurrence is $d_n = A \\cdot 1^n + \\sum_{i} P_i(n)r_i^n$ where $|r_i|<1$.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $d_n \\to A$. Since $(d_n)$ is a sequence of integers, it must be eventually constant. Let $d_n = d$ for all $n \\ge N_0$ for some $N_0 \\ge N'$.\nSince the sequence $(a_n)$ is strictly increasing for $n \\ge N'$, $d \\ge 1$.",
        "Step 3: So for $n \\ge N_0$, $a_n$ is an arithmetic progression with common difference $d \\ge 1$.\nThe polynomial $P(x)$ must satisfy the original equation for these values of $n$. For $n \\ge N_0$, $a_{n+j} = a_n+jd$.\nThe relation $P(a_n) = a_{n+1} \\cdots a_{n+k}$ becomes $P(a_n) = \\prod_{j=1}^k (a_n+jd)$.\nLet $Q(x) = \\prod_{j=1}^k (x+jd)$. Then $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are two polynomials that agree on the infinite set $\\{a_{N_0}, a_{N_0+1}, \\ldots\\}$. Therefore, the polynomials must be identical, $P(x) = Q(x)$.\n$P(x) = (x+d)(x+2d)\\cdots(x+kd)$.\nThis polynomial is monic of degree $k$. As $d \\ge 1$ is an integer, its coefficients are positive integers. So this is a valid polynomial $P(x)$.\n\nThe relation $P(a_n) = a_{n+1} \\cdots a_{n+k}$ must hold for all $n \\ge 1$. We have established that for some $d \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$, $P(x) = \\prod_{j=1}^k (x+jd)$ and $a_n$ is an arithmetic progression with common difference $d$ for $n \\ge N_0$.\nLet's check for $n=N_0-1$.\n$P(a_{N_0-1}) = a_{N_0} a_{N_0+1} \\cdots a_{N_0+k-1}$.\nUsing $P(x) = \\prod_{j=1}^k (x+jd)$, the left side is $\\prod_{j=1}^k (a_{N_0-1}+jd)$.\nThe right side is $\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} a_{N_0+j} = \\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (a_{N_0}+jd)$.\nSo we must have $\\prod_{j=1}^k (a_{N_0-1}+jd) = \\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (a_{N_0}+jd)$.\nLet $x=a_{N_0-1}$. The roots of the polynomial $\\prod_{j=1}^k(x+jd)$ are $-d, -2d, \\ldots, -kd$.\nLet $y=a_{N_0}$. The roots of $\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(y+jd)$ are $0, -d, \\ldots, -(k-1)d$.\nThe equation is $\\prod_{j=1}^k (x+jd) = \\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (y+jd)$. Let this polynomial be $R(z)$.\nThe roots of $R(z-x)$ must match the roots of $R(z-y)$. Wait, this is not a polynomial identity.\nThis is an equality of two numbers.\nLet $a_{N_0} = a_{N_0-1} + d_{N_0-1}$.\nThe equation is $\\prod_{j=1}^k (a_{N_0-1}+jd) = \\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (a_{N_0-1}+d_{N_0-1}+jd)$.\nLet $Z = a_{N_0-1}$. The equation is $(Z+d)(Z+2d)\\cdots(Z+kd) = (Z+d_{N_0-1})(Z+d_{N_0-1}+d)\\cdots(Z+d_{N_0-1}+(k-1)d)$.\nLet $f(t) = (t+d)(t+2d)\\cdots(t+kd)$ and $g(t) = (t+d_{N_0-1})(t+d_{N_0-1}+d)\\cdots(t+d_{N_0-1}+(k-1)d)$.\nWe have $f(Z)=g(Z)$.\nConsider $f(t)-g(t)$. This is a polynomial of degree at most $k-1$.\n$f(t) = t^k + d\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} t^{k-1} + \\dots$\n$g(t) = t^k + (kd_{N_0-1} + d\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}) t^{k-1} + \\dots$\nThe coefficient of $t^{k-1}$ must be equal, so $d\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} = kd_{N_0-1} + d\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}$.\n$d\\frac{k}{2}(k+1-(k-1)) = kd_{N_0-1}$, so $dk=kd_{N_0-1}$, which means $d=d_{N_0-1}$.\nThis means $a_{N_0}-a_{N_0-1}=d$.\nBy backward induction, $a_{n+1}-a_n=d$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\nSo the sequence must be an arithmetic progression from the start.\n$a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d$ for some integers $a_1, d$.\nSince $a_n$ are positive integers for all $n \\ge 1$, we must have $a_1 \\ge 1$.\nAnd since the sequence is strictly increasing, $d \\ge 1$.\nFor such a sequence, $P(a_n)=\\prod_{j=1}^k(a_n+jd)$ and $a_{n+1}\\cdots a_{n+k} = \\prod_{j=1}^k a_{n+j} = \\prod_{j=1}^k(a_1+(n+j-1)d) = \\prod_{j=1}^k(a_n+jd)$. The condition holds for all $n$.\nSo, all arithmetic progressions $a_n=a_1+(n-1)d$ with $a_1 \\ge 1, d \\ge 1$ are solutions.",
        "Step 4: Combining the results from both cases, the sequences are:\n1. Constant sequences: $a_n=a$ for any positive integer $a$. In this case, the polynomial is $P(x)=x^k$.\n2. Arithmetic progressions: $a_n=a_1+(n-1)d$ for any positive integers $a_1$ and $d$. In this case, for a given sequence with common difference $d$, the polynomial is $P(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^k(x+jd)$.\n\nThese two sets describe all possible sequences."
