      "question": "C1. Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers greater than 1 . In each unit square of an $m \\times n$ grid lies a coin with its tail-side up. A move consists of the following steps:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n  \\item select a $2 \\times 2$ square in the grid;\n  \\item flip the coins in the top-left and bottom-right unit squares;\n  \\item flip the coin in either the top-right or bottom-left unit square.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nDetermine all pairs ( $m, n$ ) for which it is possible that every coin shows head-side up after a finite number of moves.",
      "steps": [
        "Step 0: Let the grid be represented by an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with entries in $\\mathbb{F}_2 = \\{0, 1\\}$. Let a coin with its tail-side up be represented by 0 and head-side up by 1. The initial state is the zero matrix, $A_0 = \\mathbf{0}$. The target state is the matrix of all ones, $A_f = \\mathbf{J}$.\n\nFlipping a coin corresponds to adding 1 (modulo 2) to the corresponding entry in the matrix.\nA move is defined for a $2 \\times 2$ square. Let's denote the top-left corner of such a square by its coordinates $(i, j)$, where $1 \\le i \\le m-1$ and $1 \\le j \\le n-1$.\nA move consists of flipping the coins at $(i, j)$ (top-left), $(i+1, j+1)$ (bottom-right), and either $(i, j+1)$ (top-right) or $(i+1, j)$ (bottom-left).\n\nLet's analyze the two types of operations for the square at $(i, j)$:\n1.  Type 1 (TR-flip): Flip coins at $(i, j), (i+1, j+1), (i, j+1)$. This corresponds to adding the matrix $M_{i,j}^{(1)}$ which has 1s at these positions and 0s elsewhere.\n2.  Type 2 (BL-flip): Flip coins at $(i, j), (i+1, j+1), (i+1, j)$. This corresponds to adding the matrix $M_{i,j}^{(2)}$ which has 1s at these positions and 0s elsewhere.\n\nLet $u_{i,j}$ be the number of times we apply the TR-flip on square $(i,j)$ and $v_{i,j}$ be the number of times we apply the BL-flip. Since we are working over $\\mathbb{F}_2$, we can consider $u_{i,j}, v_{i,j} \\in \\{0, 1\\}$. The final state of the grid is given by the matrix $A = \\sum_{i=1}^{m-1} \\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} (u_{i,j} M_{i,j}^{(1)} + v_{i,j} M_{i,j}^{(2)})$. We want to determine for which $(m,n)$ there exist $u_{i,j}, v_{i,j}$ such that $A = \\mathbf{J}$.\n\nThe state of a cell $(k,l)$ is flipped if it's part of the three flipped coins of an operation. A cell $(k,l)$ is:\n- Top-left of the square $(k,l)$. It is flipped in both Type 1 and Type 2 moves on $(k,l)$. Contribution: $u_{k,l}+v_{k,l}$.\n- Top-right of the square $(k,l-1)$. It is flipped in a Type 1 move on $(k,l-1)$. Contribution: $u_{k,l-1}$.\n- Bottom-left of the square $(k-1,l)$. It is flipped in a Type 2 move on $(k-1,l)$. Contribution: $v_{k-1,l}$.\n- Bottom-right of the square $(k-1,l-1)$. It is flipped in both Type 1 and Type 2 moves on $(k-1,l-1)$. Contribution: $u_{k-1,l-1}+v_{k-1,l-1}$.\n\nThe final state $A_{k,l}$ of the cell $(k,l)$ is the sum of these contributions. We require $A_{k,l}=1$ for all $1 \\le k \\le m, 1 \\le l \\le n$. The variables $u_{i,j}, v_{i,j}$ are defined for $1 \\le i \\le m-1, 1 \\le j \\le n-1$. We can extend their definition to be 0 for any index outside this range.\n$A_{k,l} = (u_{k,l}+v_{k,l}) + u_{k,l-1} + v_{k-1,l} + (u_{k-1,l-1}+v_{k-1,l-1}) = 1$.\nThis gives a system of $mn$ linear equations in $2(m-1)(n-1)$ variables over $\\mathbb{F}_2$. A solution exists if and only if the matrix $\\mathbf{J}$ is in the linear span of the move matrices $\\{M_{i,j}^{(1)}, M_{i,j}^{(2)}\\}$.",
        "Step 1: Let $V = M_{m,n}(\\mathbb{F}_2)$ be the vector space of all $m \\times n$ matrices over $\\mathbb{F}_2$. Let $W$ be the subspace of $V$ spanned by the move matrices. We want to know when $\\mathbf{J} \\in W$.\nBy the principles of linear algebra, $\\mathbf{J} \\in W$ if and only if $\\mathbf{J}$ is orthogonal to the orthogonal complement of $W$, denoted $W^\\perp$.\nThe space $V$ is equipped with an inner product $\\langle A, B \\rangle = \\sum_{k,l} A_{k,l}B_{k,l} \\pmod 2$.\n$W^\\perp = \\{ B \\in V \\mid \\langle M, B \\rangle = 0 \\text{ for all } M \\in W \\}$.\nThis is equivalent to $\\langle M_{i,j}^{(1)}, B \\rangle = 0$ and $\\langle M_{i,j}^{(2)}, B \\rangle = 0$ for all $1 \\le i \\le m-1, 1 \\le j \\le n-1$.\n\nThe condition $\\langle M_{i,j}^{(1)}, B \\rangle = 0$ means $B_{i,j} + B_{i,j+1} + B_{i+1,j+1} = 0$.\nThe condition $\\langle M_{i,j}^{(2)}, B \\rangle = 0$ means $B_{i,j} + B_{i+1,j} + B_{i+1,j+1} = 0$.\nThese equations must hold for all $i \\in [1,m-1], j \\in [1,n-1]$.\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives $B_{i,j+1} - B_{i+1,j} = 0$, so $B_{i,j+1} = B_{i+1,j}$ for all $i \\in [1,m-1], j \\in [1,n-1]$.\nThis condition implies that the value of $B_{k,l}$ depends only on the sum $k+l$. Let $B_{k,l} = c_{k+l}$ for some sequence $c_s$ where $s=k+l$ ranges from $2$ to $m+n$.\n\nSubstituting $B_{k,l}=c_{k+l}$ into the first equation:\n$c_{i+j} + c_{i+j+1} + c_{i+1+j+1} = c_{i+j} + c_{i+j+1} + c_{i+j+2} = 0$.\nLet $s=i+j$. This recurrence relation $c_s + c_{s+1} + c_{s+2} = 0$ must hold for all $s$ that can be represented as $i+j$ with $1 \\le i \\le m-1, 1 \\le j \\le n-1$. The minimum value of $s$ is $1+1=2$ and the maximum is $(m-1)+(n-1)=m+n-2$.\nSo, any matrix $B \\in W^\\perp$ is of the form $B_{k,l}=c_{k+l}$, where the sequence $(c_s)$ satisfies $c_s + c_{s+1} + c_{s+2} = 0$ for $s=2, 3, \\dots, m+n-2$.",
        "Step 2: The condition for the existence of a solution is that $\\langle \\mathbf{J}, B \\rangle = 0$ for all $B \\in W^\\perp$.\n$\\langle \\mathbf{J}, B \\rangle = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n B_{k,l} = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n c_{k+l} = 0$.\nThis must hold for any sequence $(c_s)_{s=2}^{m+n}$ satisfying the recurrence relation $c_{s+2} = c_s+c_{s+1}$ for $s=2, \\dots, m+n-2$.\nSuch a sequence is entirely determined by its first two values, $c_2$ and $c_3$. The space of such sequences is a 2-dimensional vector space over $\\mathbb{F}_2$. Thus, we need the condition to hold for a basis of this space.\nLet's choose a basis:\n1. The sequence corresponding to $(c_2, c_3) = (1, 0)$.\n2. The sequence corresponding to $(c_2, c_3) = (0, 1)$.\n\nThe recurrence $c_{s+2}=c_s+c_{s+1}$ implies that the sequence is periodic with period 3 for $s \\ge 2$:\n$c_2, c_3, c_2+c_3, c_2, c_3, c_2+c_3, \\dots$. So $c_{s+3}=c_s$ for $s \\ge 2$.\nThe condition $\\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n c_{k+l} = 0$ must hold for any choice of $c_2, c_3$.\n\nLet's compute this sum. Let $C(m,n) = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n c_{k+l}$.\nLet $S_i(p) = \\sum_{j=1}^p c_{i+j}$. Due to the periodicity of $c_s$, for $p \\ge 1$:\n$\\sum_{j=1}^3 c_{i+j} = c_{i+1}+c_{i+2}+c_{i+3} = c_{i+1}+c_{i+2}+(c_{i}+c_{i+1})$ is not correct.\n$c_{s+3}=c_s$ holds for $s \\ge 2$. So $c_{j+1}+c_{j+2}+c_{j+3} = 0$ is not true.\nThe recurrence is $c_{j}+c_{j+1}+c_{j+2}=0$. So $c_{j+1}+c_{j+2}+c_{j+3}=c_{j+1}+c_{j+2}+(c_{j+1}+c_{j+2})=0$. This is true for $j \\ge 2$.\nLet's analyze the sum $C(m,n)$ carefully.\nLet $m=3k+s$ and $n=3l+r$ where $s,r \\in \\{0,1,2\\}$. Assume $s,r$ are not both zero.\n$C(m,n) = \\sum_{i=1}^{3k+s} \\sum_{j=1}^{3l+r} c_{i+j}$.\nFor any $i$, $\\sum_{j=1}^{3l+r} c_{i+j} = l \\sum_{j=1}^3 c_{i+j} + \\sum_{j=1}^r c_{i+3l+j}$.\nAs $c_s+c_{s+1}+c_{s+2}=0$ for $s \\ge 2$, the sum $\\sum_{j=1}^3 c_{i+j}$ is 0 if $i+1 \\ge 2$. This holds since $i \\ge 1$.\nSo $\\sum_{j=1}^{3l+r} c_{i+j} = \\sum_{j=1}^r c_{i+3l+j} = \\sum_{j=1}^r c_{i+j}$ (using periodicity for $i+j \\ge 2$).\nThen $C(m,n) = \\sum_{i=1}^{3k+s} \\sum_{j=1}^r c_{i+j}$.\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{3k+s} c_{i+j} = k \\sum_{i=1}^3 c_{i+j} + \\sum_{i=1}^s c_{i+3k+j} = \\sum_{i=1}^s c_{i+j}$.\nSo $C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^r \\sum_{i=1}^s c_{i+j}$.\nThis sum must be 0 for all choices of $(c_2,c_3)$.",
        "Step 3: Let's analyze $C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^{r} \\sum_{i=1}^{s} c_{i+j}$ for $m \\equiv s \\pmod 3$ and $n \\equiv r \\pmod 3$, where $s,r \\in \\{0,1,2\\}$.\nIf $s=0$ or $r=0$, the sum is empty and thus 0. This happens if $m$ or $n$ is a multiple of 3.\nIf $3 \\mid m$ or $3 \\mid n$, then $C(m,n)=0$ for any valid sequence $c$. This means a solution for the coin problem exists.\n\nNow, suppose neither $m$ nor $n$ is a multiple of 3. Then $s, r \\in \\{1, 2\\}$. We must check if $C(m,n)$ is always zero.\nCase 1: $m \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3, n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ ($s=1, r=1$).\n$C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^1 \\sum_{i=1}^1 c_{i+j} = c_2$.\nFor this to be zero for any sequence $c$, we must have $c_2=0$. But we can choose $c_2=1, c_3=0$, which defines a valid sequence. Thus $C(m,n)$ is not always zero. No solution is guaranteed. In this case, for $c_2=1, c_3=0$, $\\langle \\mathbf{J}, B \\rangle=1 \\ne 0$, so $\\mathbf{J} \\notin W$.\n\nCase 2: $m \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3, n \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$ ($s=1, r=2$).\n$C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^2 \\sum_{i=1}^1 c_{i+j} = c_{1+1} + c_{1+2} = c_2+c_3$.\nWe can choose $(c_2, c_3) = (1,0)$, making the sum 1. So it is not always zero.\n\nCase 3: $m \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3, n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ ($s=2, r=1$).\n$C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^1 \\sum_{i=1}^2 c_{i+j} = c_{1+1} + c_{2+1} = c_2+c_3$.\nThis is not always zero.\n\nCase 4: $m \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3, n \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3$ ($s=2, r=2$).\n$C(m,n) = \\sum_{j=1}^2 \\sum_{i=1}^2 c_{i+j} = (c_{1+1}+c_{2+1}) + (c_{1+2}+c_{2+2}) = c_2+c_3+c_3+c_4 = c_2+c_4$.\nUsing the recurrence, $c_4=c_2+c_3$. So $C(m,n) = c_2+(c_2+c_3)=c_3$.\nWe can choose $(c_2, c_3) = (0,1)$, making the sum 1. So it is not always zero.\n\nIn all cases where neither $m$ nor $n$ is a multiple of 3, we can find a matrix $B \\in W^\\perp$ such that $\\langle \\mathbf{J}, B \\rangle \\neq 0$. Therefore, $\\mathbf{J} \\notin W$, and it is not possible to make all coins heads up.\n\nThe problem states that $m,n > 1$.\nThus, it is possible to turn all coins to head-side up if and only if $m$ is a multiple of 3 or $n$ is a multiple of 3.",
        "Step 4: Final conclusion: The set of pairs $(m,n)$ for which it is possible for every coin to show head-side up are those positive integers $m,n > 1$ such that $m$ is a multiple of 3 or $n$ is a multiple of 3.\nThis can be written as the set $\\{(m,n) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\mid m > 1, n > 1, \\text{ and } (3 \\mid m \\text{ or } 3 \\mid n)\\}$."
      ],
      "error_steps": null,
      "failure": "context_overflow",
      "n_input_tokens": 4269,
      "context_limit": 4096
    },
    "14": {
      "idx": "hard2verify:math-2024-IMO-SL-QA-A4-gemini-2.5-pro",
      "source_idx": "",
      "classification": null,
      "category": "hard2verify",
      "question": "A4. Let $\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ be the set of all positive integers. Determine all subsets $\\mathcal{S}$ of $\\left\\{2^{0}, 2^{1}, 2^{2}, \\ldots\\right\\}$ for which there exists a function $f: \\mathbb{Z}_{>0} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{S}=\\left\\{f(a+b)-f(a)-f(b) \\mid a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\right\\}\n$$",
      "steps": [
        "Step 0: Let $\\mathcal{P} = \\left\\{2^{0}, 2^{1}, 2^{2}, \\ldots\\right\\}$. We are looking for all subsets $\\mathcal{S} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}$ for which there exists a function $f: \\mathbb{Z}_{>0} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ such that $\\mathcal{S}=\\left\\{f(a+b)-f(a)-f(b) \\mid a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\right\\}$.\nLet $d(a, b) = f(a+b) - f(a) - f(b)$. The set of all values of $d(a,b)$ is $\\mathcal{S}$.\nNote that $d(a,b) = d(b,a)$ since addition is commutative.\nAlso, since the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{Z}_{>0} \\times \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, which is non-empty, the set $\\mathcal{S}$ must be non-empty.",
        "Step 1: Consider the expression $f(a+b+c)$ for $a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. We can compute this in two ways using the associativity of addition.\n$f((a+b)+c) = f(a+b) + f(c) + d(a+b, c) = (f(a)+f(b)+d(a,b)) + f(c) + d(a+b, c) = f(a)+f(b)+f(c)+d(a,b)+d(a+b,c)$.\n$f(a+(b+c)) = f(a) + f(b+c) + d(a, b+c) = f(a) + (f(b)+f(c)+d(b,c)) + d(a, b+c) = f(a)+f(b)+f(c)+d(b,c)+d(a,b+c)$.\nEquating these two expressions gives the following relation for all $a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$:\n$$d(a,b)+d(a+b,c) = d(b,c)+d(a,b+c) \\quad (*)$$",
        "Step 2: The elements of $\\mathcal{S}$ are powers of 2. Let $x, y, z, w \\in \\mathcal{S}$ be powers of 2. If $x+y=z+w$, we can analyze the equality in binary. This implies that the multiset $\\{x, y\\}$ must be equal to the multiset $\\{z, w\\}$.\nTo see this, assume $x=2^p, y=2^q, z=2^r, w=2^s$. Without loss of generality, let $p \\ge q$ and $r \\ge s$. The equation is $2^q(2^{p-q}+1) = 2^s(2^{r-s}+1)$. The term in the parentheses is odd. By uniqueness of prime factorization, we must have $q=s$. This implies $2^{p-q}+1 = 2^{r-s}+1$, which gives $p-q=r-s$, so $p=r$. Thus, $\\{2^p, 2^q\\} = \\{2^r, 2^s\\}$.\nFrom relation $(*)$, we have $\\{d(a,b), d(a+b,c)\\} = \\{d(b,c), d(a,b+c)\\}$ for all $a,b,c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$.",
        "Step 3: Let's show that $|\\mathcal{S}| \\le 2$.\nSuppose a function $f$ exists. Define a sequence $g: \\mathbb{Z}_{>0} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{S}$ by $g(n) = d(n,1)$.\nA simple induction shows that $f(n)$ can be expressed in terms of $g$.\n$f(2) = f(1+1) = 2f(1) + d(1,1) = 2f(1) + g(1)$.\n$f(3) = f(2+1) = f(2)+f(1)+d(2,1) = (2f(1)+g(1)) + f(1) + g(2) = 3f(1) + g(1)+g(2)$.\nIn general, for $n>1$, $f(n) = n f(1) + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} g(i)$. Let $f(1)$ be a positive integer. We can choose $f(1)$ large enough, e.g., $f(1)=1$, such that $f(n) = n + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} g(i) \\ge n+(n-1) = 2n-1 \\ge 1$ for $n \\ge 1$ (since $g(i) \\in \\mathcal{S}$, $g(i) \\ge 1$). So $f(n) \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ for all $n$.\n\nFor a function of this form, we can calculate the general term $d(a,b)$:\n$d(a,b) = f(a+b)-f(a)-f(b)$\n$= \\left((a+b)f(1) + \\sum_{i=1}^{a+b-1} g(i)\\right) - \\left(af(1) + \\sum_{i=1}^{a-1} g(i)\\right) - \\left(bf(1) + \\sum_{i=1}^{b-1} g(i)\\right)$\n$= \\sum_{i=1}^{a+b-1} g(i) - \\sum_{i=1}^{a-1} g(i) - \\sum_{i=1}^{b-1} g(i) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} g(j) - \\sum_{j=1}^{b-1} g(j)$.\nThis expression must be an element of $\\mathcal{S}$ for all $a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$.",
        "Step 4: Let's test this condition for $b=2$: $d(a,2) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+1} g(j) - \\sum_{j=1}^{1} g(j) = g(a)+g(a+1)-g(1)$.\nThis value must be in $\\mathcal{S}$ for all $a \\ge 1$. Let $g(n)=2^{k_n}$ for some integers $k_n \\ge 0$.\nSo, for any $a \\ge 1$, $2^{k_a} + 2^{k_{a+1}} - 2^{k_1}$ must be a power of 2.\nLet this value be $V$. Suppose $k_a \\ne k_1$ and $k_{a+1} \\ne k_1$. Let $k_1$ be smaller than both $k_a$ and $k_{a+1}$.\n$V = 2^{k_1}(2^{k_a-k_1} + 2^{k_{a+1}-k_1} - 1)$. For $V$ to be a power of 2, the term in parentheses must be a power of 2. Let $X=k_a-k_1>0, Y=k_{a+1}-k_1>0$. Then $2^X+2^Y-1$ must be a power of 2. As $X,Y \\ge 1$, $2^X+2^Y-1$ is an odd integer greater than 1. The only odd power of 2 is $2^0=1$. So $2^X+2^Y-1=1$, which means $2^X+2^Y=2$. Since $X,Y \\ge 1$, this is impossible.\nOther cases, e.g., $k_1$ not being the minimum, lead to a negative value or a similar contradiction unless two of the exponents are equal.\nSo, for $2^{k_a} + 2^{k_{a+1}} - 2^{k_1}$ to be a power of 2, we must have $k_a=k_1$ or $k_{a+1}=k_1$.\nThis means that for any $a \\ge 1$, we must have $g(a)=g(1)$ or $g(a+1)=g(1)$.\nLet $C=g(1)$. Then any two consecutive terms in the sequence $g$ cannot both be different from $C$.",
        "Step 5: Now suppose $|\\mathcal{S}| \\ge 3$. Let $C, S_1, S_2$ be three distinct elements in $\\mathcal{S}$. We can set $g(1)=C$. Since $\\mathcal{S}$ is the range of $d(a,b)$ and $g(n)=d(n,1)$, the range of $g$ is a subset of $\\mathcal{S}$. For $\\mathcal{S}$ to be the full set of values, the range of $g$ must contain all elements of $\\mathcal{S}$ (or be constructed carefully). Let's assume there exists a sequence $g$ with $C,S_1,S_2$ in its range.\nWe can construct a sequence $g$ that satisfies the condition ($g(n)=C$ or $g(n+1)=C$ for all $n$) and contains $S_1, S_2$. For instance, let $g(1)=C, g(2)=S_1, g(3)=C, g(4)=S_2, g(5)=C$. This sequence is valid so far.\nLet's calculate $d(3,3)$ for this sequence:\n$d(3,3) = \\sum_{j=3}^{5} g(j) - \\sum_{j=1}^{2} g(j) = g(3)+g(4)+g(5) - (g(1)+g(2))$.\n$d(3,3) = C+S_2+C - (C+S_1) = C+S_2-S_1$.\nThis value must be in $\\mathcal{S}$. Let $C=2^z, S_1=2^x, S_2=2^y$, where $z,x,y$ are distinct non-negative integers. As shown in the previous step, $2^z+2^y-2^x$ can only be a power of two if two of the exponents are equal. But we assumed $z,x,y$ are distinct. This is a contradiction.\nTherefore, our assumption that $|\\mathcal{S}| \\ge 3$ must be false.\nSo $\\mathcal{S}$ can have at most two elements. Since $\\mathcal{S}$ must be non-empty, the possible sizes of $\\mathcal{S}$ are 1 and 2.",
        "Step 6: Case 1: $|\\mathcal{S}|=1$. Let $\\mathcal{S} = \\{C\\}$ for some $C=2^k, k \\ge 0$.\nWe need to show there exists a function $f$ for which $\\mathcal{S}=\\{C\\}$.\nLet's try to construct one. Let $f(n) = an+b$ for some constants $a,b$.\n$d(a,b) = (a(a+b)+b)-(a a+b)-(a b+b) = -b$. We need $-b=C$, so $b=-C$. But $f(n)=an-C$ must be in $\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. For large $n$, this holds if $a>0$. For $n=1$, $a-C > 0$, so $a>C$. For example $f(n)=(C+1)n-C$. Then $f(n) \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ for all $n \\ge 1$. But $d(a,b)=-(C+1)(a+b)+C + (C+1)a-C+(C+1)b-C=-C$. This function works.\nAnother construction: Let $g(n)=C$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. Let $f_1=1$.\nThen $f(n) = n + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} C = n+(n-1)C$.\nFor this $f$, $d(a,b) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} C - \\sum_{j=1}^{b-1} C = bC-(b-1)C = C$.\nThe set of values is $\\{C\\}$. This construction works for any $C=2^k, k \\ge 0$.\nThus, any singleton subset of $\\mathcal{P}$ is a possible set $\\mathcal{S}$.",
        "Step 7: Case 2: $|\\mathcal{S}|=2$. Let $\\mathcal{S} = \\{C, S\\}$ for $C=2^k, S=2^m$ with $k \\ne m$.\nWe need to show there exists a function $f$ for this $\\mathcal{S}$.\nLet's construct a suitable sequence $g$. Let $g(1)=C$. The condition $g(n)=C$ or $g(n+1)=C$ must hold.\nLet's define $g$ as an alternating sequence: $g(n) = C$ for odd $n$, and $g(n)=S$ for even $n$.\nThis sequence satisfies the condition. Let's find the set of values $d(a,b)$ for this $g$.\n$d(a,b) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} g(j) - \\sum_{j=1}^{b-1} g(j)$.\n\nThe sum $\\sum_{j=u}^{v} g(j)$ depends on the parity of $u,v$. Let $L=v-u+1$ be the number of terms.\nIf $L=2m$ is even, the sum is $mC+mS$.\nIf $L=2m+1$ is odd, the sum is $(m+1)C+mS$ if $u$ is odd, and $mC+(m+1)S$ if $u$ is even.\n\nLet's compute $d(a,b)$.\nSubcase 2.1: $b$ is odd. Let $b=2m+1$. The number of terms in the first sum is $b$, which is odd. The number of terms in the second sum is $b-1=2m$, which is even.\n$\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1} g(j) = \\sum_{j=1}^{2m} g(j) = mC+mS$.\nIf $a$ is odd, $\\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} g(j) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+2m} g(j) = (m+1)C+mS$.\nSo $d(a,b) = ((m+1)C+mS) - (mC+mS) = C$.\nIf $a$ is even, $\\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} g(j) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+2m} g(j) = mC+(m+1)S$.\nSo $d(a,b) = (mC+(m+1)S) - (mC+mS) = S$.\n\nSubcase 2.2: $b$ is even. Let $b=2m$. The number of terms in the first sum is $2m$, even. The number of terms in the second sum is $2m-1$, odd.\n$\\sum_{j=a}^{a+b-1} g(j) = \\sum_{j=a}^{a+2m-1} g(j) = mC+mS$ (regardless of parity of $a$).\n$\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1} g(j) = \\sum_{j=1}^{2m-1} g(j) = mC+(m-1)S$ (since the first term is $g(1)=C$).\nSo $d(a,b) = (mC+mS) - (mC+(m-1)S) = S$.\n\nCombining these cases, the values taken by $d(a,b)$ are always either $C$ or $S$.\nTo see that both values are achieved:\n$d(1,1) = g(1) = C$.\n$d(1,2) = g(1)+g(2)-g(1) = g(2) = S$.\nSo the set of values is indeed $\\{C, S\\}$. We can construct the function $f$ using $f(n)=n+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}g(i)$ and it will be well-defined.\nThus, any two-element subset of $\\mathcal{P}$ is a possible set $\\mathcal{S}$.",
        "Step 8: Conclusion: The subsets $\\mathcal{S}$ of $\\left\\{2^{0}, 2^{1}, 2^{2}, \\ldots\\right\\}$ for which such a function $f$ exists are the non-empty subsets of size at most two. That is, all singleton subsets and all two-element subsets of $\\left\\{2^{0}, 2^{1}, 2^{2}, \\ldots\\right\\}$."
      ],
