    "20": {
      "idx": "hard2verify:math-2024-IMO-SL-QA-A6-gemini-2.5-pro",
      "source_idx": "",
      "classification": null,
      "category": "hard2verify",
      "question": "A6. Let $a_{0}, a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots$ be an infinite strictly increasing sequence of positive integers such that for each $n \\geqslant 1$ we have\n\n$$\na_{n} \\in\\left\\{\\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2}, \\sqrt{a_{n-1} \\cdot a_{n+1}}\\right\\} .\n$$\n\nLet $b_{1}, b_{2}, \\ldots$ be an infinite sequence of letters defined as\n\n$$\nb_{n}= \\begin{cases}A, & \\text { if } a_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}\\right) \\\\ G, & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nProve that there exist positive integers $n_{0}$ and $d$ such that for all $n \\geqslant n_{0}$ we have $b_{n+d}=b_{n}$.",
      "steps": [
        "Step 0: Let the given recurrence be $a_{n} \\in\\left\\{\\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2}, \\sqrt{a_{n-1} \\cdot a_{n+1}}\\right\\}$. Let $d_n = a_n - a_{n-1}$ for $n \\ge 1$. Since $(a_n)$ is a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers, $d_n$ is a positive integer for all $n \\ge 1$.\n\nIf $b_n = A$, then $a_n = \\frac{a_{n-1}+a_{n+1}}{2}$, which implies $a_{n+1} = 2a_n - a_{n-1}$. The terms $a_{n-1}, a_n, a_{n+1}$ form an arithmetic progression.\nThe difference $d_{n+1} = a_{n+1} - a_n = (2a_n - a_{n-1}) - a_n = a_n - a_{n-1} = d_n$.\n\nIf $b_n = G$, then $a_n = \\sqrt{a_{n-1} a_{n+1}}$, which implies $a_{n+1} = \\frac{a_n^2}{a_{n-1}}$. The terms $a_{n-1}, a_n, a_{n+1}$ form a geometric progression. Since all $a_n$ are integers, the common ratio $r = a_n/a_{n-1}$ must be a rational number. Let $r = p/q$ where $p,q$ are coprime positive integers. Since the sequence is strictly increasing, $p>q \\ge 1$.\n$a_n = a_{n-1} \\frac{p}{q} \\implies q a_n = p a_{n-1}$. As $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, $a_{n-1}$ must be a multiple of $q$, so $a_{n-1}=cq$ for some integer $c$. This gives $a_n=cp$.\n$a_{n+1} = a_n \\frac{p}{q} = cp \\frac{p}{q} = \\frac{cp^2}{q}$. Since $a_{n+1}$ is an integer and $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, $q$ must divide $c$. Let $c=mq$ for some positive integer $m$.\nThen, $a_{n-1} = mq^2$, $a_n = mqp$, and $a_{n+1} = mp^2$.\nThe difference $d_{n+1} = a_{n+1} - a_n = mp^2 - mqp = mp(p-q)$.\nAnd $d_n = a_n - a_{n-1} = mqp - mq^2 = mq(p-q)$.\nSo, $d_{n+1} = \\frac{p}{q} d_n = \\frac{a_n}{a_{n-1}} d_n$.",
        "Step 1: Let $g_n = \\gcd(a_{n-1}, a_n)$ for $n \\ge 1$.\nIf $b_n=A$, then $g_{n+1} = \\gcd(a_n, a_{n+1}) = \\gcd(a_n, 2a_n - a_{n-1}) = \\gcd(a_n, -a_{n-1}) = \\gcd(a_n, a_{n-1}) = g_n$.\nIf $b_n=G$, with $a_{n-1}=mq^2, a_n=mqp, a_{n+1}=mp^2$ as above:\n$g_n = \\gcd(mq^2, mqp) = mq\\gcd(q,p) = mq$.\n$g_{n+1} = \\gcd(mqp, mp^2) = mp\\gcd(q,p) = mp$.\nSo, $g_{n+1} = \\frac{p}{q} g_n = \\frac{a_n}{a_{n-1}} g_n$.\n\nLet's consider the quantity $I_n = \\frac{d_n}{g_n} = \\frac{a_n-a_{n-1}}{\\gcd(a_{n-1}, a_n)}$.\nIf $b_n=A$, $d_{n+1}=d_n$ and $g_{n+1}=g_n$, so $I_{n+1}=I_n$.\nIf $b_n=G$, $d_{n+1}=\\frac{p}{q}d_n$ and $g_{n+1}=\\frac{p}{q}g_n$, so $I_{n+1} = \\frac{(p/q)d_n}{(p/q)g_n} = I_n$.\nThus, $I_n$ is an invariant of the sequence for $n \\ge 1$. Let $I_n=I$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n$I = \\frac{a_n-a_{n-1}}{\\gcd(a_{n-1}, a_n)}$. Let $k_{n-1} = a_{n-1}/g_n$ and $k_n' = a_n/g_n$. Then $\\gcd(k_{n-1}, k_n')=1$.\n$I = \\frac{g_n k_n' - g_n k_{n-1}}{g_n} = k_n' - k_{n-1}$.\nSo for each $n \\ge 1$, we have $a_{n-1} = g_n k_{n-1}$ and $a_n = g_n(k_{n-1}+I)$, where $\\gcd(k_{n-1}, k_{n-1}+I)=1$, which is equivalent to $\\gcd(k_{n-1}, I)=1$.\nLet's define a state of the system at step $n$ by the pair $S_n=(g_n, k_{n-1})$.",
        "Step 2: Let's analyze the transitions of the state $S_n=(g_n, k_{n-1})$. Let $k_n = a_n/g_{n+1}$.\nIf $b_n=A$: $a_{n+1}=2a_n-a_{n-1} = 2g_n(k_{n-1}+I) - g_n k_{n-1} = g_n(k_{n-1}+2I)$.\n$g_{n+1} = \\gcd(a_n, a_{n+1}) = \\gcd(g_n(k_{n-1}+I), g_n(k_{n-1}+2I)) = g_n\\gcd(k_{n-1}+I, I)$.\nSince $\\gcd(k_{n-1},I)=1$, we have $\\gcd(k_{n-1}+I,I)=1$. So $g_{n+1}=g_n$.\n$k_n = a_n/g_{n+1} = a_n/g_n = k_{n-1}+I$.\nThe new state is $S_{n+1}=(g_n, k_{n-1}+I)$. We must check that $\\gcd(k_n, I)=1$, which is $\\gcd(k_{n-1}+I, I)=\\gcd(k_{n-1}, I)=1$. This holds.\n\nIf $b_n=G$: $a_{n+1} = a_n^2/a_{n-1} = (g_n(k_{n-1}+I))^2 / (g_n k_{n-1}) = g_n(k_{n-1}+I)^2/k_{n-1}$.\nFor $a_{n+1}$ to be an integer, $k_{n-1}$ must divide $g_n(k_{n-1}+I)^2$. Since $\\gcd(k_{n-1}, k_{n-1}+I)=1$, we must have $k_{n-1}|g_n$.\n$g_{n+1} = \\gcd(a_n, a_{n+1}) = \\gcd(g_n(k_{n-1}+I), g_n(k_{n-1}+I)^2/k_{n-1}) = \\frac{g_n(k_{n-1}+I)}{k_{n-1}}\\gcd(k_{n-1}, k_{n-1}+I) = g_n \\frac{k_{n-1}+I}{k_{n-1}}$.\n$k_n = a_n/g_{n+1} = g_n(k_{n-1}+I) / (g_n\\frac{k_{n-1}+I}{k_{n-1}}) = k_{n-1}$.\nThe new state is $S_{n+1}=(g_n\\frac{k_{n-1}+I}{k_{n-1}}, k_{n-1})$. We check $\\gcd(k_n,I)=\\gcd(k_{n-1},I)=1$.\nSo, the state transitions are:\n$S_n=(g,k) \\xrightarrow{b_n=A} (g, k+I)$. This is always possible.\n$S_n=(g,k) \\xrightarrow{b_n=G} (g\\frac{k+I}{k}, k)$, only if $k|g$.",
        "Step 3: Let's analyze the sequence of states $S_n=(g_n, k_{n-1})$. Let's write $k_{n-1}$ as $K_n$.\nThe sequence $K_n$ is non-decreasing: $K_{n+1} = K_n+I$ if $b_n=A$ and $K_{n+1}=K_n$ if $b_n=G$.\nThe sequence $g_n$ is non-decreasing: $g_{n+1}=g_n$ if $b_n=A$ and $g_{n+1}=g_n\\frac{K_n+I}{K_n} > g_n$ if $b_n=G$.\n\nCase 1: There are only a finite number of $G$'s in the sequence $b_n$. Let $b_{n_0-1}$ be the last $G$. For $n \\ge n_0$, $b_n=A$.\nThen for $n \\ge n_0$, $S_{n+1}=(g_{n_0}, K_{n_0}+ (n-n_0)I)$.\nFor $b_n=G$ to be possible, we need $K_n|g_n$. For $n \\ge n_0$, this would be $K_{n_0}+(n-1-n_0)I | g_{n_0}$. As $n \\to \\infty$, the left side grows linearly, while the right side is constant. So for $n$ large enough, this condition cannot be met. This means $b_n$ MUST be $A$ for large $n$.\nSo for $n \\ge n_1$ for some $n_1$, $b_n=A$. This sequence is eventually constant, so it is eventually periodic with period $d=1$.\n\nCase 2: There are only a finite number of $A$'s in the sequence $b_n$. Let $b_{n_0-1}$ be the last $A$. For $n \\ge n_0$, $b_n=G$.\nFor $n \\ge n_0$, $K_{n+1}=K_n$, so $K_n = K_{n_0}$ for all $n \\ge n_0$. Let $K^*=K_{n_0}$.\nAlso, for $n \\ge n_0$, $g_{n+1} = g_n \\frac{K^*+I}{K^*}$. For $g_n$ to be an integer for all $n \\ge n_0$, we must have $K^*=1$.\nThe condition $K_n|g_n$ for $b_n=G$ becomes $1|g_n$, which is always true.\nSo the sequence can be eventually all $G$'s. This is periodic with period $d=1$.\n\nCase 3: There are infinitely many $A$'s and infinitely many $G$'s.\nThe presence of infinitely many $A$'s implies $K_n \\to \\infty$. Thus there exists $n_0$ such that for all $n \\ge n_0$, $K_n > 1$.\nA sequence of two consecutive $G$'s, $b_n=G, b_{n+1}=G$, requires special conditions.\nFor $b_n=G$, we need $K_n|g_n$. State becomes $(g_n\\frac{K_n+I}{K_n}, K_n)$.\nFor $b_{n+1}=G$, the state is $(g_{n+1},K_{n+1})=(g_n\\frac{K_n+I}{K_n}, K_n)$. We need $K_{n+1}|g_{n+1}$, i.e., $K_n | g_n\\frac{K_n+I}{K_n}$. As $\\gcd(K_n, K_n+I)=\\gcd(K_n,I)=1$, this implies $K_n | (g_n/K_n)$, which means $K_n^2|g_n$.\nMore generally, for a sequence of $p$ consecutive $G$'s starting at $n$, we need $K_n^p | g_n$.\nThe common ratio of the resulting geometric progression of $a_i$ must be an integer for the $g_i$ sequence to remain integer, which implies $K_n=1$.\nTo be precise, if $b_n=G, b_{n+1}=G$, then $a_{n-1},a_n,a_{n+1},a_{n+2}$ form a geometric progression. Let the ratio be $r=p/q$. For $a_k$ to be integers, we found $a_{n-1}=mq^3, a_n=mq^2p, \\ldots$. $g_n=\\gcd(a_{n-1}, a_n)=mq^2$, $K_n=a_{n-1}/g_n = q$. Similarly, $g_{n+1}=mqp$, $K_{n+1}=a_n/g_{n+1}=q$. For $g_{n+2}$ to be an integer, we must have $q=1$. So $K_n=1$.\nSince $K_n \\to \\infty$, there is an $n_0$ such that $K_n > 1$ for all $n \\ge n_0$.\nThus, for $n \\ge n_0$, there cannot be two consecutive $G$'s. Every $G$ must be followed by an $A$.",
        "Step 4: Let $n_1 < n_2 < n_3 < \\ldots$ be the indices greater than $n_0$ for which $b_n=G$.\nFor $j \\ge 1$, we have $b_{n_j}=G$. Since there are no consecutive G's, we must have $b_{n_j+1}=A$.\nLet $m_j = n_{j+1}-n_j-1 \\ge 0$ be the number of $A$'s between $b_{n_j+1}$ and $b_{n_{j+1}-1}$.\nLet $c_j = g_{n_j}/K_{n_j}$. This is an integer, because $b_{n_j}=G$ is possible only if $K_{n_j}|g_{n_j}$.\nThe state at $n_j$ is $S_{n_j}=(c_j K_{n_j}, K_{n_j})$.\n$b_{n_j}=G$: $S_{n_j+1} = (c_j(K_{n_j}+I), K_{n_j})$.\nThen there are $m_j+1$ A's until the next G at $n_{j+1}$.\n$K_{n_{j+1}} = K_{n_j} + (m_j+1)I$.\n$g_{n_{j+1}} = g_{n_j+1} = c_j(K_{n_j}+I)$.\nThe condition for $b_{n_{j+1}}=G$ is $K_{n_{j+1}}|g_{n_{j+1}}$.\n$K_{n_j} + (m_j+1)I | c_j(K_{n_j}+I)$.\nThen $c_{j+1} = g_{n_{j+1}}/K_{n_{j+1}} = \\frac{c_j(K_{n_j}+I)}{K_{n_j}+(m_j+1)I}$.\nSince $j$ is large enough ($n_j > n_0$), we have $m_j+1 \\ge 1$.\n$c_{j+1} = c_j \\frac{K_{n_j}+I}{K_{n_j}+(m_j+1)I}$.\nAs $I>0$, $K_{n_j}+(m_j+1)I \\ge K_{n_j}+I$. Equality holds if $m_j=0$.\nSo $c_{j+1} \\le c_j$. The sequence of positive integers $(c_j)$ is non-increasing.\nTherefore, it must eventually become constant. Let $c_j=c^*$ for $j \\ge j_0$.\nFor $c_{j+1}=c_j$ to hold, we must have $K_{n_j}+I = K_{n_j}+(m_j+1)I$, which implies $(m_j+1)I=I$, so $m_j+1=1$, which means $m_j=0$.\n$m_j = n_{j+1}-n_j-1 = 0 \\implies n_{j+1}-n_j=1$. This contradicts the fact that there are no consecutive $G$'s for $n>n_0$. Let's re-examine $m_j$.\n\nNo, $b_{n_j+1}, \\ldots, b_{n_{j+1}-1}$ are A's. Number of these A's is $n_{j+1}-1-(n_j+1)+1=n_{j+1}-n_j-1=m_j$.\nAfter $b_{n_j}=G$, the k-value is $K_{n_j+1}=K_{n_j}$.\nThen $m_j$ A's follow. $K_{n_j+2}=K_{n_j}+I, \\ldots, K_{n_{j+1}}=K_{n_j}+m_j I$.\n$g_{n_{j+1}}=g_{n_j+1}=c_j(K_{n_j}+I)$.\nCondition for $b_{n_{j+1}}=G$ is $K_{n_{j+1}}|g_{n_{j+1}}$, i.e., $K_{n_j}+m_j I | c_j(K_{n_j}+I)$.\n$c_{j+1} = g_{n_{j+1}}/K_{n_{j+1}} = \\frac{c_j(K_{n_j}+I)}{K_{n_j}+m_j I}$.\nAs shown, $K_n>1$ for large $n$, so $n_{j+1}-n_j > 1$, meaning $m_j \\ge 1$.\nThen $K_{n_j}+m_j I > K_{n_j}+I$ if $m_j>1$.\nThis implies $c_{j+1}<c_j$ if $m_j>1$.\nThe sequence $c_j$ cannot decrease infinitely often. So, there is a $j_0$ such that for all $j \\ge j_0$, we have $m_j=1$.\nThis means that for $n \\ge n_{j_0}$, the G's are separated by exactly one A.\nThe sequence of indices of G's is $n_{j_0}, n_{j_0}+2, n_{j_0}+4, \\ldots$.\nThis means for $n \\ge n_{j_0}$, the sequence $b_n$ is $G, A, G, A, \\ldots$.\nThis sequence is periodic with period $d=2$.\nAll cases lead to an eventually periodic sequence for $b_n$."
      ],
